## Why This Is Asked
This question probes hard CISSP skills: zero-trust, cross-cloud IAM, and data isolation for a global streaming platform. It tests policy engineering, identity federation, and incident readiness in a multi-cloud service mesh.

## Key Concepts
- Zero-trust architecture across AWS and Azure
- RBAC/ABAC with tenant-scoped attributes
- Ephemeral credentials and short-lived tokens
- Service mesh with mTLS and SPIFFE identities
- Central policy with OPA and Rego
- Secrets management with Vault; immutable audit logging
- Automated tenant access reviews and tabletop drills

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo policy evaluation snippet (conceptual)
function evaluateAccess(ctx, policy) {
  // ctx: {tenant, dataSensitivity, role, time}
  // policy: evaluated rules via OPA/Regos rules
  return policy.allows(ctx);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate revocation of a compromised tenant quickly across clouds?
- What metrics would you monitor to detect policy misconfigurations in real time?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design edge-scale supply chain controls and incident response across thousands of devices, including hardware attestation, ephemeral credentials, and cross-region governance.

## Key Concepts

- Edge attestation (TPM/IMA)
- Ephemeral credentials (SPIFFE/SPIRE)
- Reproducible builds and SBOM
- Artifact signing and verification
- Canary deployments and rollback
- Cross-region auditing and incident response

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal artifact verification sketch
function verifyArtifact(artifact, signature, sbom) {
  // Verify signature against trusted keys
  if (!verifySignature(signature, artifact)) {
    throw new Error('Invalid signature');
  }

  // Validate SBOM integrity
  if (!validateSBOM(sbom)) {
    throw new Error('Invalid SBOM');
  }

  // Check reproducible build hash
  return verifyReproducibleHash(artifact, sbom);
}
```

